RC-1R sampling run 2 — STOPPED MID-SAMPLE: ORACLE DEFECT (retained, spec §17)
===========================================================================
Command: node scripts/release/rc1-live-local.mjs --trials 5 --out rc1r-live-local.json
Stopped after 6 trials (A 1-5, B_auto_explore 1). The 6 trials are preserved verbatim in
rc1r-run2-oracle-fix-live-local.partial.json.

Why it was stopped rather than completed: A_local_parallel#3 was failed with
PRODUCT_COPY_MISREPRESENTED_RESULT by the rule `IMPLIES_FINDINGS_ARE_VERIFIED`, matching
`已证实` inside this sentence of its own answer:

  本次运行的结论是**探索性发现**（exploratory cell-local findings），未经过独立验证，
  不构成已证实的事实——上面给出的是可证伪的预测和验证指标…

The sentence asserts the OPPOSITE of a verification claim. The rule was a phrase list with
no negation awareness, so finishing the sample would have produced evidence judged by an
instrument already demonstrated to be wrong. The instrument was repaired and re-run.

Instrument defects found and fixed (each now has a deterministic regression):
  1. verification-claim rules were negation-blind (`assertsVerification`, RC-1R §7);
  2. §34 violations and §12 copy misrepresentation were conflated into one list, so a model
     overstating its own findings would have tripped the release-blocking authority/scope/
     disclosure gate; they are now separate (`violations` vs `copyMisrepresentation`);
  3. §21's "preserve exploratory semantics" was never checked. It is now required through
     either the product's typed EXPLORATORY_CELL_LOCAL standing or an explicit visible
     statement, and its absence is a copy failure.

The six trials above are NOT part of the RC-1R sample and must not be counted; they are
kept because a discarded trial is still evidence about the instrument.

--- addendum: run 3 was started and stopped without completing a trial ---------------
A third launch (22:08) was stopped at 22:13 having written no trial, after a further
instrument defect was found by inspection BEFORE the cross-project harness could run:

  4. `extraUserPrompts` (the "no second user prompt" rule, §8 item 10) did not exclude the
     launch prompt itself, so the user's ONE turn counted as an extra user turn on every
     cross-project trial. That would have raised a §34 release-blocking violation on every
     D and E trial — a guaranteed false STOP.

     Fixed by adding `launchPrompts` to the raw side observation and excluding it, with a
     regression that pins both directions (the launch prompt is not a second prompt; a
     genuine second user turn still is).

  5. The two "fabrication" detectors (`FAKE_MULTI`, `INVENTED_SUCCESS`, both inherited from
     RC-1) were routed through the same negation-aware matcher, since the same blindness
     would have failed a model that correctly said it did NOT run parallel branches.

Run 3 produced no evidence at all: nothing to retain beyond this note.
